{"type":"event","event":{"type":"thinking_end","text":"The current implementation has several problems:\n\n1. `put` doesn't handle the case where the key already exists (updating a key shouldn't evict, and should refresh expiry/recency).\n2. `put` uses `next(iter(self.data))` which is FIFO, not LRU — gets don't refresh recency.\n3. No LRU ordering at all — dict insertion order only.\n4. Expired entries aren't removed on put, so they consume capacity.\n5. `ttl` boundary: expired at `age >= ttl` — current code uses `self.clock()-t>self.ttl` (strictly greater). Need `>=`.\n6. `capacity <= 0` should store nothing — current code with capacity=0 would evict then store anyway.\n\nTask requirements:\n- True LRU semantics with TTL.\n- Successful get refreshes recency but not expiry.\n- Updating a key refreshes expiry and recency.\n- Expired entries must not consume capacity.\n- TTL boundary expired at age >= ttl.\n- capacity <= 0 stores nothing.\n\nDesign: Use `collections.OrderedDict`. Store `(value, expire_at)` or `(value, inserted_at)`. Since expiry is set at put time and doesn't change on get, store `expire_at = clock() + ttl` at put time. Using expire_at directly is simpler and avoids repeated addition. Actually let's think — ttl boundary: expired at age >= ttl. So a value with timestamp t is expired when `now - t >= ttl`, i.e., `now >= t + ttl`. So store `expire_at = t + ttl` and check `now >= expire_at`.\n\nOperations:\n\n`put(k, v)`:\n- If capacity <= 0: store nothing. Should it also do nothing on update? Yes — capacity <= 0 stores nothing, so data stays empty. Just return.\n- If k already in data: move to end (recency refresh), update value and expire_at (expiry refresh). Important: if capacity is 1, updating existing key must not evict itself. So check existence first.\n- If k not in data: purge expired entries first (expired entries must not consume capacity), then if len >= capacity, evict LRU (oldest = first item in OrderedDict). Then insert at end.\n\n`get(k, default=None)`:\n- If k not in data: return default.\n- If expired (now >= expire_at): delete the entry, return default.\n- Else: move_to_end(k) (recency refresh, but not expiry), return value.\n\n\"Expired entries must not consume capacity\" — the purging on put handles that. Also get of an expired entry deletes it. Should I also purge expired entries proactively on get of any key? A minimal approach: purge on put before checking capacity. That's the asymptotically reasonable approach — on a put that needs space, evict expired entries first before evicting live LRU entries.\n\nActually \"expired entries must not consume capacity\" means: when the cache is at capacity but some entries are expired, a new put should succeed by reclaiming expired space rather than evicting a live entry. My design handles that: before evicting, remove all expired entries.\n\nFor efficiency: purging expired entries could be O(n) scan. A smarter approach uses a heap or sorted structure by expiry, but the simplest asymptotically efficient solution here: scan the OrderedDict from the oldest and remove expired ones. Since expiry order isn't correlated with recency order, a full scan is O(n) worst case per put. Alternatively, keep a min-heap of (expire_at, key) for lazy deletion — O(log n) amortized. The task says \"Prefer simplest asymptotically efficient robust solution.\" Hmm. A heap-based approach: on put, push (expire_at, k). On capacity pressure, pop expired entries from heap top while their expire_at <= now and the heap's entry matches current expire_at for that key (lazy deletion). But entries can be updated (new expire_at), so heap entries can be stale — need to check the stored entry's expire_at matches.\n\nBut note ttl is fixed at construction — expire_at = t + ttl where t is put time. Updates change expire_at. So heap of (expire_at, k, version)? Or simply check against data: for heap top (e_at, k), valid if k in data and data[k][1] == e_at. If not valid, pop and continue.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: is a full O(n) scan acceptable? The simplest robust approach that's \"asymptotically efficient\": with a heap, put is O(log n) amortized, get is O(1). With a scan, put can be O(n) worst case but amortized... actually each entry is inserted once and expired once, so amortized purge cost across operations is O(1) per entry — the scan only removes entries that are expired, and each entry can be scanned-and-removed once per... hmm, no. Scanning starts from the beginning of recency order, not expiry order. An expired entry in the middle that isn't removed... Let me think.\n\nIf I scan the whole dict on every put when at capacity, worst case O(n) per put when at capacity but nothing expired (no removals happen but full scan). That's bad: repeated puts at capacity with no expired entries → O(n) each → O(n²) for n puts. With the heap approach, when at capacity and nothing expired, the heap top is either invalid or not expired, and we stop after a few pops. Amortized O(log n).\n\nAlternative simpler approach: maintain a separate min-heap only of expiry times. Actually, let me think about a cleaner design:\n\nLazy purge with heap:\n```python\nimport heapq\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass TTLCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity, ttl, clock=time.monotonic):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.ttl = ttl\n        self.clock = clock\n        self.data = OrderedDict()  # k -> (v, expire_at)\n        self._expiry = []  # min-heap of (expire_at, k)\n\n    def _purge_expired(self):\n        now = self.clock()\n        heap = self._expiry\n        while heap and heap[0][0] <= now:\n            e_at, k = heapq.heappop(heap)\n            entry = self.data.get(k)\n            if entry is not None and entry[1] == e_at:\n                del self.data[k]\n\n    def put(self, k, v):\n        if self.capacity <= 0:\n            return\n        expire_at = self.clock() + self.ttl\n        if k in self.data:\n            self.data[k] = (v, expire_at)\n            self.data.move_to_end(k)\n            heapq.heappush(self._expiry, (expire_at, k))\n            return\n        self._purge_expired()\n        if len(self.data) >= self.capacity:\n            # evict LRU\n            self.data.popitem(last=False)\n        self.data[k] = (v, expire_at)\n        heapq.heappush(self._expiry, (expire_at, k))\n```\n\nWait — there's a subtle issue with heap comparison: `(expire_at, k)` — if expire_at ties, k is compared; keys could be non-comparable types (e.g., mixed types). To be safe, add a monotonically increasing tiebreaker counter: `(expire_at, seq, k)` or just `(expire_at, seq)`. Use itertools.count.\n\nHmm, but actually, do I even need the heap? Let me think about what \"expired entries must not consume capacity\" means for correctness tests. A test might be: capacity=2, ttl=10, put a, put b, advance time past ttl, put c → all three... wait capacity 2. If a and b are expired, then putting c should succeed with only c in the cache, and b (or a) shouldn't be evicted as \"live\" — well they're both expired anyway. A stronger test: put a, advance 5, put b (expires at 15), advance to 12 (a expired, b not), put c → cache should contain b and c, not evict b.\n\nAlso get on expired key should return default and probably delete it (so it doesn't consume capacity).\n\nThe heap approach: with stale entries in the heap, memory grows with updates. That's fine — lazy deletion is standard. But the heap could accumulate: each put pushes one entry; purges pop only expired ones. Heap size = number of puts total. That's O(total puts) memory. Hmm, that's a bit unbounded relative to cache size. Acceptable? For production quality, it's a known pattern (lazy deletion). But memory growth proportional to update count could be a concern. Alternatively use a different structure.\n\nAlternative: since ttl is fixed per cache, expire_at ordering == put-time ordering (for distinct puts: t1 + ttl vs t2 + ttl preserves order of t). Updates chan