rn default\n        if self.clock() >= entry[1]:\n            del self.data[k]  # expired: reclaim it\n            return default\n        self.data.move_to_end(k)  # refresh recency, not expiry\n        return entry[0]\n```\n\nWait — there's a bug: entry could be None if value stored is None? `entry = self.data.get(k)` returns the tuple `(v, expire_at)` — a tuple, never None (get returns None only for missing key). Values can be None and it's fine since we check the tuple. Good.\n\nHmm, but one issue: `self.data.get(key)` in _purge_expired — same, tuple. Fine.\n\nWait, another subtlety in put when key exists: I push a new heap entry for the updated expire_at; the old heap entry becomes stale — handled by the `entry[1] == expire_at` validity check. Good.\n\nBut there's a memory concern: each put pushes an entry; stale entries are popped when their expire_at <= now. Stale entries from updates have expire_at <= current entry's — they surface first. Over time, heap holds entries not yet expired. Fine.\n\nAnother subtlety: purge pops heap entries with expire_at <= now, and for each checks validity. A key could have multiple stale entries; each gets popped and skipped. Good.\n\nEdge: capacity check `len(self.data) >= self.capacity` after purge — if capacity is, say, 3 and we have 2 live → insert without evict. Good. Note purge only runs when inserting a new key and before capacity check. Should purge run even when not at capacity? Not needed — expired entries consuming memory but not blocking. But \"expired entries must not consume capacity\" strictly means len(data) could exceed... no: len(data) <= capacity always (we evict before insert). Expired entries within data count toward len but purge-at-put-time reclaims before evicting live ones. Hmm, but consider: capacity 2, put a (expire 10), put b (expire 20). At t=11, a is expired but still in data. len(data) = 2 = capacity. put c → purge removes a → len 1 → insert c → len 2. b retained. Correct.\n\nBut what if a test ch