t). Then insert at end.\n\n`get(k, default=None)`:\n- If k not in data: return default.\n- If expired (now >= expire_at): delete the entry, return default.\n- Else: move_to_end(k) (recency refresh, but not expiry), return value.\n\n\"Expired entries must not consume capacity\" — the purging on put handles that. Also get of an expired entry deletes it. Should I also purge expired entries proactively on get of any key? A minimal approach: purge on put before checking capacity. That's the asymptotically reasonable approach — on a put that needs space, evict expired entries first before evicting live LRU entries.\n\nActually \"expired entries must not consume capacity\" means: when the cache is at capacity but some entries are expired, a new put should succeed by reclaiming expired space rather than evicting a live entry. My design handles that: before evicting, remove all expired entries.\n\nFor efficiency: purging expired entries could be O(n) scan. A smarter approach uses a heap or sorted structure by expiry, but the simplest asymptotically efficient solution here: scan the OrderedDict from the oldest and remove expired ones. Since expiry order isn't correlated with recency order, a full scan is O(n) worst case per put. Alternatively, keep a min-heap of (expire_at, key) for lazy deletion — O(log n) amortized. The task says \"Prefer simplest asymptotically efficient robust solution.\" Hmm. A heap-based approach: on put, push (expire_at, k). On capacity pressure, pop expired entries from heap top while their expire_at <= now and the heap's entry matches current expire_at for that key (lazy deletion). But entries can be updated (new expire_at), so heap entries can be stale — need to check the stored entry's expire_at matches.\n\nBut note ttl is fixed at construction — expire_at = t + ttl where t is put time. Updates change expire_at. So heap of (expire_at, k, version)? Or simply check against data: for heap top (e_at, k), valid if k in data and data[k][1] == e_at. If not valid, pop and continue.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: is a full O(n) scan acceptable? The simplest robust approach that's \"asymptotically efficient\": with a heap, put is O(log n) amortized, get is O(1). With a scan, put can be O(n) worst case but amortized... actually each entry is inserted once and expired once, so amortized purge cost across operations is O(1) per entry — the scan only removes entries that are expired, and each entry can be scanned-and-removed once per... hmm, no. Scanning starts from the beginning of recency order, not expiry order. An expired entry in the middle that isn't removed... Let me think.\n\nIf I scan the whole dict on every put when at capacity, worst case O(n) per put when at capacity but nothing expired (no removals happen but full scan). That's bad: repeated puts at capacity with no expired entries → O(n) each → O(n²) for n puts. With the heap approach, when at capacity and nothing expired, the heap top is either invalid or not expired, and we stop after a few pops. Amortized O(log n).\n\nAlternative simpler approach: maintain a separate min-heap only of expiry times. Actually, let me think about a cleaner design:\n\nLazy purge with heap:\n```python\nimport heapq\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass TTLCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity, ttl, clock=time.monotonic):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.ttl = ttl\n        self.clock = clock\n        self.data = OrderedDict()  # k -> (v, expire_at)\n        self._expiry = []  # min-heap of (expire_at, k)\n\n    def _purge_expired(self):\n        now = self.clock()\n        heap = self._expiry\n        while heap and heap[0][0] <= now:\n            e_at, k = heapq.heappop(heap)\n            entry = self.data.get(k)\n            if entry is not None and entry[1] == e_at:\n                del self.data[k]\n\n    def put(self, k, v):\n        if self.capacity <= 0:\n            return\n        expire_at = self.clock() + self.ttl\n        if k in self.data:\n