if capacity=2 and one entry is expired, putting two new keys should not evict anything (only the expired one is purged, plus one more slot free... let me think: capacity=2, entry a is expired. put b: purge a → len 0. insert b. put c: len 1 < 2, insert. Now both b and c present, a gone.) If we didn't purge on put: put b → len(data)=1 (a expired but counted) >= 2? No, 1 < 2 → insert. put c → len=2 >= 2 → evict LRU which is a (expired, oldest) → fine actually. Hmm, in that case eviction would remove the expired entry anyway since expired entries are typically oldest in recency order... but not necessarily! An expired entry could have been recently updated. E.g., capacity=2: put a at t=0 ttl=10. put b at t=5. At now=12, a is expired (age 12), b age 7 not expired. put c at t=12: without purge, len=2 → evict LRU = a → insert c. Works by luck. But: put a at t=10, put b at t=11, now=15: a expired? age 5, no. Let me construct: entries a (t=100) and b (t=105), ttl=10. At now=111: a age 11 expired, b age 6 fresh. put c: without purge len=2, evict LRU=a. Insert c. Same result as purge. Hmm, when could no-purge evict a non-expired entry while an expired one exists? Expired entry means age >= ttl. LRU order is recency. If entry x is expired (updated at t_x, now - t_x >= ttl) and entry y is fresh and y is LRU-before x... y was accessed more recently than x was put/updated (if put updates recency and get refreshes recency). y fresh means now - t_y < ttl where t_y is y's last put/update time. But y's recency could be refreshed by get at time t_g > t_x. y's expiry is based on t_y (put time), not recency. So: put y at t=0 (ttl=10), get y at t=9 (recency 9). put x at t=9.5. At now=10.2: y age 10.2 >= 10 → expired. x age 0.7 fresh. LRU order: x (0.5... wait x at 9.5, y recency 9) → LRU is y (recency 9 < 9.5). y expired → evict y. Still fine.\n\nTry: put x at t=0, ttl=10. put y at t=9. At now=10.5: x expired (age 10.5), y age 1.5 fresh. LRU: x (0) < y (9). Evict x. Fine.\n\nHmm, is it a 