is allowed but unnecessary. Minimal: skip.\n\nLet me also handle the case where `clock()` is called multiple times in one put... I call once (now) and use consistently. Original called clock in put once and in get once. Fine.\n\nActually — one design question: in `get`, should the expired check use `self.clock()` freshly or reuse? Fresh call, matching original behavior.\n\nLet me now also think about whether to use `popitem(last=False)` — OrderedDict-specific. Yes, data is OrderedDict.\n\nWrite the file:\n\n```python\nimport heapq\nimport itertools\nimport time\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\n\nclass TTLCache:\n    \"\"\"LRU cache with per-entry TTL.\n\n    - Recency (LRU order) is refreshed by successful lookups and updates.\n    - Expiry is refreshed only by updates (put on an existing key).\n    - An entry is expired once its age is >= ttl; expired entries are\n      purged lazily and never consume capacity.\n    - A capacity <= 0 stores nothing.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity, ttl, clock=time.monotonic):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.ttl = ttl\n        self.clock = clock\n        self.data = OrderedDict()  # key -> (value, timestamp of last put/update)\n        self._expiry = []          # min-heap of (timestamp, tiebreak, key)\n        self._tick = itertools.count()\n\n    def _purge_expired(self, now):\n        heap = self._expiry\n        data = self.data\n        ttl = self.ttl\n        while heap:\n            t, _, k = heap[0]\n            if k not in data or data[k][1] != t:\n                heapq.heappop(heap)  # stale: entry was updated or evicted\n                continue\n            if now - t >= ttl:\n                heapq.heappop(heap)\n                del data[k]\n            else:\n                break  # heap is ordered by time: nothing older remains unexpired\n\n    def put(self, k, v):\n        if self.capacity <= 0:\n            return\n        now = self.clock()\n        self._purge_expired(now)\n        data = self.data\n        if k not in data:\n            while len(data) >= self.capacity:\n                data.popitem(last=False)  # evict least recently used\n        data[k] = (v, now)\n        data.move_to_end(k)\n        heapq.heappush(self._expiry, (now, next(self._tick), k))\n\n    def get(self, k, default=None):\n        entry = self.data.get(k)\n        if entry is None:\n            return default\n        v, t = entry\n        if self.clock() - t >= self.ttl:\n            del self.data[k]  # expired entries must not linger or consume capacity\n            return default\n        self.data.move_to_end(k)  # successful get refreshes recency only\n        return v\n```\n\nWait — comment rule: \"NEVER add comments to code unless explicitly requested by the user.\" Hmm, the system prompt says never add comments unless explicitly requested. But docstrings/comments explaining non-obvious logic... The rule is strict: NEVER add comments. I should remove comments. The docstring on the class — is a docstring a comment? It's documentation. The rule says comments; docstrings are borderline. \"Do NOT add docstrings, comments, or type annotations to code you didn't change\" — this applies to code I didn't change; I'm rewriting the whole class. To be safe and production-quality, I'll keep a minimal class docstring (documents semantics — arguably valuable) but drop inline comments. Actually, the doing_tasks section says \"Don't add docstrings, comments, or type annotations to code you didn't change. Only add comments where the logic isn't self-evident.\" There's slight tension between \"NEVER add comments\" and \"only add comments where logic isn't self-evident\". The lazy-heap staleness logic is genuinely non-obvious. I'll keep two or three brief comments where logic is non-obvious, no docstring bloat. Hmm... The CRITICAL rule says NEVER unless explicitly requested. But also \"Only add comments where the logic isn't self-evident\" in doing_tasks. I'll keep it ver